# =====================================================================================
#  03 - The working setup behind Application Gateway, APIM in internal mode
#  https://{{agwHost}}/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}    and    https://{{agwHost2}}/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
#
#  The same sign-in as 02-working-proxy.http, but the client only ever talks to Application
#  Gateway on a custom domain. APIM has no public endpoint (docs/APP-GATEWAY.md).
#
#    client --TLS <host>--> App Gateway --(path rule: MCP paths only)--> APIM (internal) --> Azure DevOps
#    browser ------------------------------------------------------------> Microsoft Entra ID
#
#  Two hosts on the same App Gateway and the same APIM instance (APP-GATEWAY.md section 9):
#    {{agwHost}}   custom host ALSO configured on APIM. App Gateway sends Host: <host>, so APIM
#                  builds every URL from the request host. Nothing is rewritten.     Part A
#    {{agwHost2}}  custom domain ONLY on App Gateway. App Gateway sends
#                  Host: <apim-name>.azure-api.net and X-Forwarded-Host: <host2>, and rewrites
#                  that internal host to <host2> in the 401 challenge. The PRM and OAuth
#                  policies take the host from X-Forwarded-Host (trustForwardedFor = true). Part B
#  Part C shows what App Gateway does NOT let through.
#
#  Rule of the whole flow: every URL a client is given (resource_metadata, resource,
#  authorization_servers, issuer, authorize/token/register endpoints) must be on the host
#  the client connected to. A client cannot reach <apim-name>.azure-api.net (internal), and
#  RFC 9728 section 3.3 makes it reject a PRM whose "resource" differs from its URL.
#
#  DCR and the Entra app: see "Client registration (DCR) and the Entra app" in http/README.md.
#  Steps A5/A6 (browser, code) are manual, as in file 02; see http/README.md.
#
#  Variables (environment file): agwHost, agwHost2 (both https://...), org, clientId, token.
#  App Gateway must be running: az network application-gateway start -g <rg> -n <app-gateway>
# =====================================================================================

# Fixed, lab-only PKCE pair (RFC 7636). Never reuse a fixed verifier in a real client.
@codeVerifier = lab-only-pkce-verifier-0123456789-abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
@codeChallenge = zaFp8oSoZN9fqMAEJGn4XTBBL02a9LeZix3gER7YRB4
@redirectUri = http://localhost
@mcpUrl = {{agwHost}}/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
@mcpUrl2 = {{agwHost2}}/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
# Paste the "code" from the browser address bar after step A5 (manual, valid ~10 minutes).
@authCode = paste-the-code-here
# Token for the MCP calls. Default: the az token from your environment file. After a
# successful step A6, use {{a6token.response.body.$.access_token}} instead.
@bearer = {{token}}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART A - <host>: custom host on App Gateway AND on APIM
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

###
# STEP A1 - Anonymous initialize
# PATH  : App Gateway listener "https", rule "mcp" (path /ado-mcp-proxy/*) -> APIM pool,
#         Host: <host> -> APIM MCP server ado-mcp-proxy -> Azure DevOps answers 401.
# EXPECT: HTTP 401, no body:
#           WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/<org>"
#         APIM builds this URL from the Host header. App Gateway sent the public host, so
#         the URL is already right; App Gateway does not touch it on this host.
# LOGS  : appgw-access.kql (RuleName mcp, OriginalHost = Host = <host>, ServerStatus 401).
# @name a1init
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough-agw","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP A2 - Protected-resource metadata (API ado-mcp-proxy-prm, oauth-prm.xml)
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "resource": "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/<org>",                     <- the URL the client used
#             "authorization_servers": [ "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth" ],     <- the gateway, not Entra
#             "scopes_supported": [ "https://mcp.dev.azure.com/.default" ], ... }
#         Never <apim-name>.azure-api.net.
# @name a2prm
GET {{agwHost}}/ado-mcp-proxy/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/{{org}}
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP A3 - Authorization-server metadata, path-inserted alias (RFC 8414)
# PATH  : /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/ado-mcp-proxy/* is one of the alias paths
#         App Gateway routes to APIM (APP-GATEWAY.md section 6). Codex, Claude Code and
#         Copilot CLI fetch this address; without the route they cannot sign in.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "issuer": "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth",
#             "authorization_endpoint": "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth/authorize",
#             "token_endpoint": "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth/token",
#             "registration_endpoint": "https://<host>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth/register", ... }
# @name a3asMetadata
GET {{agwHost}}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP A4 - Dynamic client registration (oauth-register.xml)
# WHY   : MCP clients register themselves (RFC 7591). Entra has no registration endpoint, so
#         APIM answers every client with the SAME pre-registered Entra public client. Nothing
#         is created in Entra; Entra still enforces the app's redirect URIs at sign-in.
# EXPECT: HTTP 201
#           { "client_id": "<public-client-id>", "redirect_uris": [ "http://localhost" ],
#             "token_endpoint_auth_method": "none", ... }
#         client_id equals {{clientId}} in your environment file, the same on every host:
#         App Gateway changes nothing about the app registration.
# @name a4register
POST {{a3asMetadata.response.body.$.registration_endpoint}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"client_name":"http-walkthrough-agw","redirect_uris":["{{redirectUri}}"],"grant_types":["authorization_code","refresh_token"],"response_types":["code"],"token_endpoint_auth_method":"none"}

###
# STEP A5 - Authorization request (oauth-authorize.xml)
# WHY   : The client sends resource = its MCP URL. APIM drops it and adds
#         resource=https://mcp.dev.azure.com, the identifier Entra accepts with the Azure
#         DevOps scope. Without this, Entra answers AADSTS9010010.
# EXPECT: HTTP 302, Location: https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/oauth2/v2.0/authorize?client_id=<public-client-id>
#           &redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost&scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
#           &code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256&state=walkthrough-agw
#           &resource=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com
#         The browser goes straight to Entra. App Gateway is not on the sign-in path, and
#         Entra does not need to know any gateway host name.
# MANUAL: as file 02 step 5: open the Location in a browser, sign in, copy "code" from
#         http://localhost/?code=...&state=walkthrough-agw into @authCode.
# @name a5authorize
# @no-redirect
GET {{a3asMetadata.response.body.$.authorization_endpoint}}
    ?client_id={{a4register.response.body.$.client_id}}
    &response_type=code
    &redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
    &scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
    &resource={{mcpUrl}}
    &code_challenge={{codeChallenge}}
    &code_challenge_method=S256
    &state=walkthrough-agw

###
# STEP A6 - Token request (oauth-token.xml)  [needs @authCode from step A5]
# EXPECT: HTTP 200 with the user's access token for Azure DevOps MCP. With the placeholder
#         code: 400 invalid_grant (AADSTS9002313), which still proves APIM reached Entra
#         through App Gateway with resource replaced (not invalid_target / AADSTS9010010).
# @name a6token
POST {{a3asMetadata.response.body.$.token_endpoint}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code
&client_id={{a4register.response.body.$.client_id}}
&code={{authCode}}
&redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
&code_verifier={{codeVerifier}}
&resource={{mcpUrl}}

###
# STEP A7 - Authenticated initialize
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, text/event-stream (data: {"result":{"serverInfo":{"name":"AzureDevOps.Mcp"...}}}).
#         The first byte arrives as soon as Azure DevOps sends it: response buffering is
#         off on App Gateway (APP-GATEWAY.md section 7).
# @name a7initAuth
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough-agw","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP A8 - tools/call (read-only)
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE with a project list.
# LOGS  : appgw-access.kql (ClientIp = your IP); in APIM the rate limit uses the same IP,
#         the last X-Forwarded-For entry (APP-GATEWAY.md section 8).
# @name a8toolsCall
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"core_list_projects","arguments":{"top":5}}}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART B - <host2>: custom domain only on App Gateway, APIM on <apim-name>.azure-api.net
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

###
# STEP B1 - Anonymous initialize, challenge rewritten by App Gateway
# PATH  : listener "https-2", rule "mcp-2" -> APIM with Host: <apim-name>.azure-api.net and
#         X-Forwarded-Host: <host2> (rewrite set mcp-headers-2).
# EXPECT: HTTP 401
#           WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://<host2>/ado-mcp-proxy/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/<org>"
#         APIM generated https://<apim-name>.azure-api.net/...; App Gateway's response
#         rewrite replaced that host with {var_host}. Without the rewrite the client would be
#         sent to an internal host it cannot resolve (APP-GATEWAY.md section 13, negative test).
# LOGS  : appgw-access.kql (RuleName mcp-2, OriginalHost <host2>, Host <apim-name>.azure-api.net).
# @name b1init
POST {{mcpUrl2}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough-agw","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP B2 - PRM built from X-Forwarded-Host
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, "resource": "https://<host2>/ado-mcp-proxy/<org>",
#         "authorization_servers": [ "https://<host2>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth" ].
#         App Gateway cannot rewrite bodies. oauth-prm.xml takes the host from the last
#         X-Forwarded-Host entry because the named value mcp-trust-forwarded-for is true.
#         With it false this shows <apim-name>.azure-api.net and clients reject the PRM.
# @name b2prm
GET {{agwHost2}}/ado-mcp-proxy/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/{{org}}
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP B3 - Authorization-server metadata on <host2>
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, issuer https://<host2>/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth and every endpoint on <host2>.
# @name b3asMetadata
GET {{agwHost2}}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/ado-mcp-proxy/oauth
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP B4 - Dynamic client registration on <host2>
# EXPECT: HTTP 201 with the same client_id as step A4.
# @name b4register
POST {{b3asMetadata.response.body.$.registration_endpoint}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"client_name":"http-walkthrough-agw2","redirect_uris":["{{redirectUri}}"],"token_endpoint_auth_method":"none"}

###
# STEP B5 - Authorization request on <host2>
# EXPECT: HTTP 302 to login.microsoftonline.com with resource=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com,
#         the same as step A5. The host the client used never reaches Entra.
# @name b5authorize
# @no-redirect
GET {{b3asMetadata.response.body.$.authorization_endpoint}}
    ?client_id={{b4register.response.body.$.client_id}}
    &response_type=code
    &redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
    &scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
    &resource={{mcpUrl2}}
    &code_challenge={{codeChallenge}}
    &code_challenge_method=S256
    &state=walkthrough-agw2

###
# STEP B6 - tools/call on <host2>
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE with a project list. The token from either host works on both:
#         its audience is Azure DevOps MCP, not a gateway host.
# @name b6toolsCall
POST {{mcpUrl2}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"core_list_projects","arguments":{"top":5}}}

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART C - what App Gateway does not let through
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

###
# STEP C1 - Forged X-Forwarded-Host
# WHY   : The policies trust X-Forwarded-Host, so only App Gateway may set it. App Gateway
#         replaces the client's value with the host the client connected to.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, "resource": "https://<host2>/ado-mcp-proxy/<org>" (not attacker.example).
# @name c1forged
GET {{agwHost2}}/ado-mcp-proxy/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/{{org}}
Accept: application/json
X-Forwarded-Host: attacker.example

###
# STEP C2 - A path that is not an MCP path
# EXPECT: HTTP 502 from App Gateway (ERRORINFO_EMPTY_BACKEND_POOL in appgw-access.kql).
#         Other APIs on the APIM instance, including its status endpoint, stay unreachable.
# @name c2unrouted
GET {{agwHost}}/status-0123456789abcdef

###
# STEP C3 - Root OAuth metadata
# EXPECT: HTTP 502. Nothing of this setup lives at the host root; clients that fall back
#         there get App Gateway's 502 instead of APIM's 404 (APP-GATEWAY.md section 6).
# @name c3rootMetadata
GET {{agwHost}}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP C4 - Regular (native) setup on <host2>: why only the working setup is usable there
# EXPECT: HTTP 200 from APIM's built-in PRM:
#           "resource": "https://<apim-name>.azure-api.net/ado-mcp-native/<org>"   <- internal host
#           "authorization_servers": [ "https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0" ]
#         APIM builds this document itself from its own host and ignores X-Forwarded-Host;
#         App Gateway cannot rewrite bodies. Clients reject it (resource != their URL).
#         On <host> the same document names <host>, and sign-in fails later at Entra with
#         AADSTS9010010 (file 01).
# @name c4nativePrm
GET {{agwHost2}}/ado-mcp-native/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/{{org}}
Accept: application/json
